\ core::iterator -- the linear iteration protocol (HKT as the associated-type
\ substitute; P7b.S8 R1). `Iterator['It: * -> *]` makes the iterator itself
\ the type constructor rather than naming an associated element type: `next`
\ consumes the iterator and yields the element plus the advanced iterator
\ packed together in one `Step` value. `Done` carries nothing, so the
\ exhausted case's final drop lives inside `next`'s own `Done` arm -- the one
\ canonical site, never a remainder construction (this is why the S6
\ construction wall, `src/check/poly.rs:6116-6117`, is never reached by any
\ shape in this module). The Option-shaped alternative
\ (`next ( 'It['T] -- Option['T] 'It['T] )`) was considered and rejected: arm
\ parity would force the dead iterator out to every caller and the final drop
\ to every `None` path instead of living in one place.
import: intrinsics | drop | ;
import: self::list | List Nil Cons | ;

\ `next` is deliberately absent from this list: a trait member's own
\ synthesized word is named `member;Trait;module;Type` (never the bare
\ member name, `src/parser.rs`'s `synth_member_word_name`), so `export:`
\ has no bare `next` to promise -- `exportable_names` only ever sees the
\ mangled spelling. A consumer imports the trait and its ctors
\ (`import: core::iterator | Step Done More Iterator | ;`) and does *not*
\ name `next`; bare `next` then resolves through trait dispatch at the
\ call site.
export: Step Done More Iterator ;

type: Step['T 'Rest]
| Done
| More 'T 'Rest
;

trait: Iterator['It: * -> *]
  : next ( 'It['T] -- Step['T 'It['T]] ) ;
;

\ The `Cons` arm destructures the matched list (`Cons>` disposes of the
\ matched cell and hands back its fields, element deepest, the still-cell-
\ wrapped remainder on top) and packs `More` with the element deepest and
\ the (unwrapped) remainder on top -- the same field order `Cons` itself
\ constructs with. The exhausted `Nil` arm consumes the matched (empty) list
\ via `drop` and returns only the `Done` shell: no remainder to construct, so
\ the S6 wall is never in play here.
impl: Iterator for List
  : next
    ~[ ( Nil ) drop Done ]
    ~[ ( Cons ) Cons> | v rest | v rest ^> More ]
    List? ;
;
